    "turn_text": "Yeah, in our understanding of why it happened. And then laying out legislative recommendations that would allow us to prevent a repetition of Cruz insurrections, political violence, and electoral sabotage moving in the future. But you're right, the public has a great hunger for individual criminal accountability here. People want to see justice in that sense. And I feel that myself, I'm part of that. And I think that there's been overwhelming evidence already put into the record that prosecutors could use at lots of different levels of government to make a case. And the judge, in the case from California, stated that he thought that it was likely that Donald Trump had committed offenses against the United States. So obviously, there's got to be a prosecution. He's entitled to a presumption of innocence. And the justice process has got to work its course. But there have been more than nine-inch cases brought already against people for assaulting federal officers for conspiring to interfere with the federal proceeding, the counting of electoral college votes, and the seditious conspiracy. So that judge in the John Eastman litigation in California said that he thought, as at least as a civil matter, that it was more likely than not that crimes had been committed.",
