    "turn_text": "Well, so on the first one, you know, about Jenny Thomas, is it of importance? I'd say it's a slender importance to the overall investigative proceeding, but it's a tremendous importance to me as a citizen and I think to all of us that there are intelligent, well educated people who have access to all the information in the world. who continue to drown their minds in propaganda and big lies in disinformation and conspiracy theory. I mean, it's just remarkable to me that you know, a well-educated woman who happens to be married to a Supreme Court justice, no less, would disregard the findings of 60 federal and state court judges, including eight nominated to bench by Donald Trump himself and simply proclaim without any evidence that the election was stolen from Trump and that he's really the president. That to me is just a matter of essential civic concern that she's representative of millions and millions of people who are thinking that way. That's a dangerous thing for an Enlightenment democracy built on the principle of reason in public places. So yeah, now look, I want to be clear, she has a First Amendment right like every other citizen to believe and spout whatever nonsense she wants. Just because she's married to a Supreme Court justice, at least in my book, doesn't mean that she can't say those utterly deranged and depraved things. She's got a right to do it. And in some sense, we're better off knowing where",
