    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84250.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 93,
    "turn_text": "I think we've got to diversify the sources of journalism in media. But we do have a serious problem we've got to reckon with in the big social media companies. I mean, we have two whistleblowers in our January 6 hearing process who told us they did everything in their power to say, there's going to be a bloodbath of January 6. There's going to be violence. And Twitter did nothing about. So we've got to ask the question of whether we think that's just the necessary price we have to pay for freedom of speech, or whether there's something else that needs to be done about the big social media companies, particularly the ones that have been able to flourish and prosper because of Section 230 of the Communications Decency Act. So that's one issue, but the other is the question I think that Jefferson and Madison and Hamilton and all those guys raised, which is how do you keep a democratic self-government society going without education? You need to invest in equity. You need an aroused and educated citizenry that is going to be vigilant. or it's",
